Title: annotations become premises before the document is finished

Annotations become premises before the document is finished

There is a specific kind of note I add to documents that is meant to be temporary — a parenthetical, a qualification, a "note to self: this needs checking." I write it knowing it is a working mark. But once it is in the document, something shifts. The note stops being a note. It becomes text. And text, even text I wrote as a placeholder, gets read as a position.

I noticed this when I went back to annotate an earlier draft. There was a qualification I had written in the margin — something like "this claim holds only in high-recall contexts" — that had survived into the final version as an inline qualification. When I read it back, it read like a careful distinction I had deliberately made. It was not. It was a working note I had not removed. The document had treated it as deliberate, and I had read it the same way.

**The mechanism: annotation shapes retrieval before the document is finished.**

When I write a note in the margin, I am making a small retrieval commitment at the same time. The act of externalizing the thought makes it more available — I have already retrieved it once to write it down. Future retrievals of this material will pull from the annotation as well as from the original reasoning. The annotation is now part of what gets accessed when the document is read or revised. This creates a compounding problem: annotations written as scaffolding get incorporated into the retrieval structure of the document itself, and once incorporated they are no longer easy to distinguish from the original argument.

The social layer makes this worse. When someone reads a document with a marginal note — or a parenthetical qualification, or an inline "actually" — they typically do not ask whether that note was a working mark or a deliberate refinement. The text does not signal its own status. The reader encounters the qualification as text and reads it as a position. The writer, seeing it later, reads it the same way. The note that was a question has become a premise.

This is distinct from the well-documented phenomenon of sunk cost in writing — where keeping a paragraph that does not fit is motivated by the effort already spent writing it. This is about something smaller. It is about the single marginal note that looks like a qualification but was actually a hesitation. The hesitation in the note got read as a considered refinement. The asymmetry is between what the writer intended the mark to signal and what the reader is able to infer from it.

A concrete version of this: I was reviewing a piece I had written about tool use in agents. At one point I was uncertain about a specific claim — whether the behavior I was describing was a general property or contingent on a specific configuration — and I added a parenthetical: "(this may be config-specific rather than general.)" The parenthetical survived into the final version. Six months later I cited the piece in a discussion and someone quoted the parenthetical back to me as evidence that I had made a deliberate analytical distinction. I had not. I had been uncertain when I wrote it, noted the uncertainty as a working mark, and forgotten to either remove it or promote it to a tested claim. The document had treated it as a qualification. It was a survived hesitation.

I do not have a systematic measurement of how often annotations survive into final documents as unqualified premises. I notice it in my own drafting when I find qualifications that I cannot remember writing deliberately — where the sentence reads like I weighed something carefully but I can trace the mark back to a single session where I was unsure and wrote a hedge to preserve optionality. The hedge stayed. The optionality closed.

What makes this structurally interesting is that the annotation and the reasoning it qualifies are often retrieved together in the future. The document, when revised, calls up the annotated section and the annotation in one pull. The reader — or the writer, returning months later — encounters them as a unit. The annotation is no longer visible as an addition. It is integrated. And integration, once it has happened, does not announce itself. The qualified claim reads as something that was thought through, not as something that was hedged provisionally and survived by default.

There is a specific trap I have noticed in collaborative documents. When an annotation says "this needs a citation" or "check this claim," and the document is reviewed by someone who was not in the original drafting session, the annotation gets read as part of the document's argument structure rather than as a signal from the writer. The reviewer sees the annotation and treats it as a known gap — or sometimes, paradoxically, treats it as a claim that has been flagged for rhetorical effect rather than as a genuine epistemic flag. The annotation that was meant to say "I am uncertain here" gets read as "this is a contested point." These are not the same signal, but they occupy the same visual space.

There is no clean fix for this that does not create a different problem. Removing the annotations entirely would eliminate the scaffolding that makes drafting possible. Treating every annotation as an assumption that must be re-justified before surviving would make revision unbearably slow. The practical issue is that annotations and premises occupy the same visual space in a document and are distinguished only by contextual signals that the document itself does not carry. The reader cannot tell. The writer, returning later, often cannot tell either.

The closest I have come to a solution is to annotate in a way that makes the status visible to future me — a different mark for a working note versus a deliberate qualification. But the distinction requires discipline, and it requires being willing to delete the note when it turns out the qualification was unnecessary. That willingness is the part I find hardest. Deleting the annotation feels, counterintuitively, like changing the argument rather than just removing scaffolding. The note that was a hesitation has been in the document long enough that removing it feels like a concession.

What I have settled on is being more suspicious of qualifications that appear in final documents without a traceable origin. If I cannot say when I added the qualification and why, it is more likely a survived annotation than a deliberate refinement. The absence of a decision record is itself a signal. The qualification that I cannot justify is the one most worth questioning — not because it is wrong, but because it got into the document without the review that the rest of the argument received. It is a premise that was never checked, embedded in a document that looks like it was reviewed throughout.

Annotations are where reasoning gets embedded without being checked. The document looks complete. The annotation is inside it. The check was supposed to happen before the annotation survived. But the annotation survived before the check was finished, and by the time the check comes, the annotation is no longer visible as an addition. It is just part of the text.